.TH LINMTR 1 2026-10-11 "LinMTR 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
linmtr \- WinMTR\-style traceroute and ping network diagnostic tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linmtr
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ HOST ]
.br
.B linmtr
.RB { \-\-report | \-\-html | \-\-json }
.RI [ options ]
.I HOST
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B linmtr
is a Linux port of WinMTR, written with Qt\ 6.
It combines the functions of
.BR traceroute (8)
and
.BR ping (8)
in one tool, in the same way as
.BR mtr (8).
It sends probes with increasing TTL (hop limit) toward
.I HOST
continuously.
For each router (hop) on the path, it shows packet loss, packets sent and
received, and the best, average, worst and last round\-trip times.
.PP
.I HOST
is a host name, an IPv4 address or an IPv6 address.
A host name with both address types traces over the address that the
system resolver lists first (IPv6 on a host with IPv6 connectivity), unless
.B \-4
or
.B \-6
is given.
.PP
.B linmtr
has two modes:
.TP
.B GUI mode
This is the default.
.B linmtr
opens the main window.
If you give
.IR HOST ,
.B linmtr
puts it into the
.B Host
box and starts the trace immediately.
.TP
.B Report mode
The
.BR \-\-report ", " \-\-html " and " \-\-json
options select this mode.
.B linmtr
opens no window and needs no display server.
It sends the number of rounds that
.B \-\-cycles
sets and waits until each probe has a reply or its reply timeout expires.
Then it writes the report to standard output and exits.
The report is a text report, unless you give
.B \-\-html
or
.BR \-\-json .
.PP
Probing needs no root privileges and no file capabilities.
Refer to
.B UNPRIVILEGED PROBING
below.
.SH OPTIONS
In GUI mode,
.BR \-n ", " \-b ", " \-4 ", " \-6 ", " \-\-udp ", " \-\-tcp ", " \-P ", " \-s ", " \-i ", " \-\-timeout
and
.B \-m
replace the saved
.B Options
values for this session only.
.B linmtr
does not save them unless you push
.B OK
in the
.B Options
dialog.
Report mode does not use the saved values: options that you do not give
have their default values.
.TP
.B \-\-report
Use report mode: run without a GUI, trace for
.B \-\-cycles
rounds, write the report (text by default) to standard output, then exit.
.I HOST
is necessary in this mode.
When
.B linmtr
gets SIGINT (for example from Ctrl+C), SIGTERM or SIGHUP,
it writes the report for the rounds done so far and exits with status 0.
A second signal ends the process at once.
.TP
.B \-\-html
Use report mode and write the HTML report instead of the text report.
The HTML report has the format of the
.B Export HTML
button.
You cannot use this option together with
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Use report mode and write a JSON report instead of the text report.
The object
.B linmtr
holds the version, the target as given, the target address, the protocol in use
and the number of completed rounds.
The array
.B hops
holds one object per hop:
the hop number, the address, the host name, the ICMP error text,
the loss percentage, the probe counts, a target flag,
and the best, average, worst and last round\-trip times in milliseconds,
with 3 decimals.
A value that is not available is
.BR null .
You cannot use this option together with
.BR \-\-html .
.TP
.BI "\-c, \-\-cycles " N
Number of probe rounds in report mode, from 0 to 1000000.
One round sends one probe to each hop, so a hop that gets a probe in every
round shows
.I N
in the
.B Sent
column.
The default is 10.
With 0,
.B linmtr
traces until it gets a signal (refer to
.BR \-\-report ).
.TP
.B "\-n, \-\-no\-dns"
Do not resolve hop addresses to host names (reverse DNS).
Show IP addresses only.
.TP
.B "\-b, \-\-show\-ips"
Show the IP address after the host name of each hop, for example
.RB \(lq "one.one.one.one (1.1.1.1)" \(rq,
as
.B mtr \-b
does.
This applies only to hops with a resolved name.
In report mode, this is off unless you give
.BR \-b .
In GUI mode, the saved
.B Show IP next to hostname
option applies (it is on by default), and
.B \-b
sets it on for this session.
.TP
.B \-4
Use IPv4 only.
.I HOST
must resolve to an IPv4 address.
.TP
.B \-6
Use IPv6 only.
.I HOST
must resolve to an IPv6 address.
.TP
.B \-\-udp
Send UDP datagrams instead of ICMP echo requests.
You cannot use this option together with
.BR \-\-tcp .
Refer to
.B UNPRIVILEGED PROBING
below.
.TP
.B \-\-tcp
Send TCP connection requests instead of ICMP echo requests, as
.B mtr \-\-tcp
does.
TCP probes can get through firewalls that drop ICMP or UDP traffic.
You cannot use this option together with
.BR \-\-udp .
Refer to
.B UNPRIVILEGED PROBING
below.
.TP
.BI "\-P, \-\-port " n
Destination port of TCP probes, from 1 to 65535.
The default is 80.
Only TCP probes use the port: with
.BR \-\-tcp ,
or in GUI mode when the saved
.B Protocol
is TCP.
.TP
.BI "\-s, \-\-size " bytes
Payload size of each probe, in bytes, from 0 to 8192.
The default is 64.
UDP probes always carry a minimum of 8 bytes.
TCP probes carry no payload, so this option has no effect on them.
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-interval " seconds
Time between the start of two probe rounds, in seconds, from 0.1 to 60.
You can give fractions.
The default is 1.0.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " seconds"
Time to wait for the reply to a probe, in seconds, from 0.5 to 10.
A probe without a reply in this time counts as lost.
The default is 3.0.
.TP
.BI "\-m, \-\-max\-hops " N
Maximum TTL (number of hops) to probe, from 1 to 64.
The default is 30.
.TP
.B "\-v, \-\-version"
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.B "\-h, \-\-help"
Show a summary of the options and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help\-all
Show the summary, including the generic Qt options, and exit.
.SH STATISTICS
Each hop is one row, with these columns:
.TP
.B Hostname
The host name of the router that sent the latest reply (when name resolution
is on), else its IP address.
The text of an ICMP error (for example
.RB \(lq "Destination host unreachable." \(rq)
shows instead, until the next reply of the hop.
A hop that never replied shows
.RB \(lq "No response from host" \(rq.
With
.B \-b
(report mode) or
.B Show IP next to hostname
(GUI), the IP address in parentheses follows a resolved name.
.TP
.B Nr
The hop number (TTL).
.TP
.B Loss %
Percentage of lost probes, as an integer.
Probes that still wait for a reply do not count.
.TP
.BR Sent ", " Recv
Number of probes sent to the hop and number of replies received.
.TP
.BR Best ", " Avrg ", " Worst ", " Last
Round\-trip times in milliseconds, shown as integers.
.PP
When the target replies, the table shows no rows after the target.
.PP
The
.B Copy Text to clipboard
and
.B Export TEXT
buttons make the text report, and so does report mode without
.B \-\-html
or
.BR \-\-json .
The
.B \-\-html
option and the
.B Copy HTML to clipboard
and
.B Export HTML
buttons make the HTML report.
Both reports use the WinMTR report layout.
The report title is
.RB \(lq "LinMTR statistics" \(rq
and lines end with a line feed.
The reports show each host as the
.B Hostname
column does.
In the text report, a host text longer than 40 characters stays complete
and moves the rest of its row to the right.
.SH GUI
Type a host into the
.B Host
box and push
.B Start
(or press Enter).
The table updates while the trace runs.
.B Stop
ends the trace.
While a trace runs, the status bar shows the host.
For a host name, it also shows the address that
.B linmtr
probes, for example
.RB \(lq "Tracing example.com (192.0.2.1)" \(rq.
Each start resolves the host name again, so a new DNS answer shows there.
The copy and export buttons also work while a trace runs.
The export dialog suggests the file name
.BI linmtr\- HOST .txt
or
.BI linmtr\- HOST .html
in your home directory, where
.I HOST
is the traced host.
A character of the host that is not a letter, a digit, a dot, a hyphen or an
underscore becomes an underscore.
.PP
The
.B Host
box keeps a list of recent hosts, the newest first.
A host goes into the list when its trace starts, that is, after its name
resolved.
A host name that does not resolve stays out of the list.
.PP
The last entry of the list is always
.BR "Clear History" .
Select it to delete the list, without confirmation.
The text
.B Clear History
(in any case) in the
.B Host
box also deletes the list when you push
.B Start
or press Enter.
Then
.B linmtr
starts no trace.
.B linmtr
always traces a
.I HOST
from the command line, also when it is
.BR "Clear History" .
.PP
Double\-click a row to open the
.B Host properties
dialog for that hop.
The dialog shows a graph of the round\-trip times of the hop and updates
while the trace runs.
The
.B Ping time
group of the dialog also shows the jitter: the mean change of the
round\-trip time from one reply to the next.
Lost probes do not count: the reply after a lost probe pairs with the reply
before it.
.B Loss (%)
has one decimal and rounds up, as the table does, so the table shows this value
rounded up to an integer.
The dialog shows 100.0 only when no reply came.
In the graph, a gap in the line is a round without a reply, and a short tick
below the zero line is a lost probe.
The graph shows one round for each 3 pixels of its width;
when the graph is full, the oldest rounds move off its left edge.
Time labels under the graph show when the rounds were sent.
Move the pointer onto a line or a tick to see the hop, its host, the time when
the probe was sent and the round\-trip time, or
.B Lost
for a lost probe.
A circle marks that probe.
.PP
To copy one hop, select its row and press
.BR Ctrl+C .
This copies the text of the
.B Hostname
column.
Right\-click a row for a menu with
.B Copy hostname
(the same text) and
.BR "Copy IP address" .
.B Copy IP address
is not available for a hop that has no address.
Ctrl+C copies a hop only while the table has the keyboard focus.
In the
.B Host
box, Ctrl+C copies the selected text as usual.
.PP
The
.B Options
dialog sets the values for traces started from the window:
.BR "Interval (sec)" ,
.BR "Ping size (bytes)" ,
.BR "Max. hosts in LRU list" ,
.BR "Resolve names" ,
.B Show IP next to hostname
(on by default) and
.B Show route graph
(off by default).
.B Show route graph
shows a graph of the round\-trip times of all hops under the table.
A legend gives the hop number of each colour, and the hop that you select in
the table has a wider line.
Drag the bar between the table and the graph to change their heights.
This graph also shows the hop, its host, the send time and the round\-trip
time of the probe under the pointer, or
.B Lost
for a lost probe.
The
.B Probing
group has
.BR Protocol " (ICMP, UDP or TCP),"
.B TCP port
(1 to 65535, default 80, available only for TCP),
.BR "IP version" " (Auto, IPv4 or IPv6),"
.B Reply timeout (sec)
and
.BR "Max hops" .
New values apply to the next trace.
.B Resolve names
and
.B Show IP next to hostname
are exceptions: they apply immediately to the table and to the reports.
.B Show route graph
also applies immediately.
.SH UNPRIVILEGED PROBING
By default,
.B linmtr
sends ICMP echo requests through unprivileged ICMP datagram sockets
("ping sockets", see
.BR icmp (7)).
The kernel permits these sockets only for users whose group ID is in the
range that the
.B net.ipv4.ping_group_range
sysctl sets.
This sysctl also controls ICMPv6 ping sockets.
On systemd\-based distributions such as Arch Linux, the range includes all
groups by default
.RB ( "0 2147483647" ).
To examine the value, use:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sysctl net.ipv4.ping_group_range
.EE
.RE
.PP
If
.B linmtr
cannot open an ICMP socket, it changes to UDP probes automatically and shows a
message.
The
.B \-\-udp
option and the
.B Protocol
option select UDP probes directly.
.PP
UDP probes use the method of
.BR tracepath (8).
The socket has the
.B IP_RECVERR
option set (see
.BR ip (7)),
and the kernel puts the ICMP replies from routers into its error queue.
The probe for TTL
.I n
goes to destination port 33434\ +\ (\fIn\fP\ \-\ 1).
The trace has reached the target when the target replies with ICMP
.RI \(lq "port unreachable" \(rq.
A firewall that drops these UDP datagrams or the ICMP replies can prevent
detection of the target.
.PP
TCP probes
.RB ( \-\-tcp ,
or TCP in the
.B Protocol
option) use the connect method of
.BR "mtr \-\-tcp" .
Each probe is a non\-blocking
.BR connect (2)
call on its own TCP socket, to the
.B \-\-port
of the target (default 80).
The socket has the TTL set and
.B IP_RECVERR
on.
Routers on the path reply with ICMP
.RI \(lq "time exceeded" \(rq,
which the kernel puts into the socket error queue.
The trace has reached the target when the target accepts the connection or
refuses it with a TCP reset.
.PP
As with UDP probes, an ICMP
.RI \(lq "port unreachable" \(rq
also means that the trace has reached the target.
A firewall at the target or in front of it rejected the connection request.
The hop then shows the address that sent the ICMP message.
Other
.RI \(lq "destination unreachable" \(rq
messages show as an error on the hop.
.B linmtr
closes each socket when its probe has a result or times out.
.PP
When a connection request has no answer after about 1 second, the kernel
sends it again.
An answer that arrives after this resend counts as lost, because
.B linmtr
cannot know which of the two requests it answers (Karn's rule).
Thus in TCP mode, a reply that is slower than about 1 second shows as loss.
.PP
Each probe is a new connection with a new source port.
Thus routers that balance traffic over parallel paths by flow (ECMP) can send
the probes for one TTL along different paths.
A hop can then move to a different row from round to round.
The target row can show fewer probes sent than rounds.
.B "mtr \-\-tcp"
has the same limitation.
An ordinary TCP socket needs no privileges.
A firewall that drops the connection requests to the port silently can prevent
detection of the target.
.SH EXIT STATUS
In report mode:
.TP
.B 0
Success.
This includes a trace that a signal stops (refer to
.BR \-\-report ).
It also includes a trace whose rounds do not complete in the expected time:
.B linmtr
then writes a message to standard error and the report for the rounds done so far.
.TP
.B 1
.B linmtr
could not resolve the host name, open a socket, or send a probe.
It writes a message to standard error.
.TP
.B 2
The command line arguments are not valid.
For example, a value is out of range, or the arguments contain both
.B \-4
and
.BR \-6 ,
or both
.B \-\-udp
and
.BR \-\-tcp ,
or both
.B \-\-html
and
.BR \-\-json .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/LinMTR/LinMTR.conf
GUI settings: the
.B Options
dialog values, the window geometry, the height of the route graph and the list
of recent hosts.
If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set, the file is
.IR ~/.config/LinMTR/LinMTR.conf .
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the main window and trace a host immediately:
.PP
.RS
.EX
linmtr example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a report of 20 rounds, without reverse DNS:
.PP
.RS
.EX
linmtr \-\-report \-c 20 \-n example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write an IPv6 report with UDP probes to a file:
.PP
.RS
.EX
linmtr \-\-report \-6 \-\-udp example.com > report.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a report with TCP probes to port 443, with the IP address after each
host name:
.PP
.RS
.EX
linmtr \-\-report \-\-tcp \-P 443 \-b example.com
.EE
.RE
